# Demonstrates Booking-to-Catalog retries, circuit breaking, recovery, and graceful degradation.
# Use the Aspire dashboard instructions in each section; every purchase body is intentionally safe for the stated experiment.
@gateway = http://localhost:5100
@booking = {{gateway}}/booking
@eventId = 2
@password = Demo@123

### Login before the outage
# @name attendeeLogin
POST {{gateway}}/identity/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "attendee@demo.com",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### Healthy baseline -> 200
GET {{gateway}}/catalog/events/{{eventId}}

### Stop Catalog in Aspire, then send once -> 503 with Retry-After after the initial GET plus 3 retries
# Inspect Booking's trace/logs for retry 1, 2, and 3 carrying the same TraceId.
POST {{booking}}/bookings
Authorization: Bearer {{attendeeLogin.response.body.$.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "eventId": {{eventId}},
  "quantity": 1,
  "simulatePaymentFailure": false
}

### Keep Catalog stopped and send again -> 503; after the fifth failed attempt the circuit is open
# Once open, repeat this request and inspect the client duration: it should finish in under 100 ms.
POST {{booking}}/bookings
Authorization: Bearer {{attendeeLogin.response.body.$.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "eventId": {{eventId}},
  "quantity": 1,
  "simulatePaymentFailure": false
}

### Graceful degradation while Catalog is stopped -> 200
GET {{booking}}/bookings/mine
Authorization: Bearer {{attendeeLogin.response.body.$.accessToken}}

### Restart Catalog, wait at least 15 seconds, then send with a fresh key -> 201
# Booking logs should show half-open followed by closed with the request TraceId.
POST {{booking}}/bookings
Authorization: Bearer {{attendeeLogin.response.body.$.accessToken}}
Idempotency-Key: step17-recovery-change-me
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "eventId": {{eventId}},
  "quantity": 1,
  "simulatePaymentFailure": false
}
